Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are well known as a class of porous materials that can offer diverse geometries and exceptionally high surface area, therefore, they are widely used in the storage, separation and release of guest molecules.[1] When come to the host of active materials as electrodes in batteries, where the fulfilment of three criteria, polarity, porosity and conductivity are required, MOFs are rarely utilized due to the their poor conductivity (Scheme 1A).[2] This also impedes their application in electronic devices.[3] Here, we combined the advantages of conductive polymer and MOFs to construct polypyrrole-MOF (ppy-MOF) compartments, thus the conductivity issue was resolved and the geometric advantages of MOFs were fully revealed. These ppy-MOF compartments were used in lithium sulfur (Li-S) batteries for the proper containment of sulfur. Exceptional high charge-discharge rate of $\mathtt{10.0C}$ ( $1.0\mathsf{C}=1685\mathsf{m A g}^{-1})$ was achieved in these MOF based batteries. Furthermore, by varying the pore geometries of MOFs, for the first time, we elucidated their critical role in accessing high rate performance.
Specifically, we synthesized three MOFs with distinct pore geometries, each of which has discrete one-dimensional channels (MIL-53), three-dimensional hierarchical nanocages (MIL-101) and cross-linked pore and tunnels (PCN-224), respectively (Figure S1).[4] Their conductivities were boosted, when constructed to form sulfur containing ppy-MOF compartments (e.g. from $10^{-5}\thinspace\mathsf{S}\thinspace\mathsf{m}^{-1}$ of the pristine MIL-53 to $10^{2}$ $\mathsf{S\ m^{-1}}$ of ppy-S-in-MIL-53). When they were tested at relatively low charge-discharge rate $(<1.0\mathrm{~\textit~{~C~}~})$ , their electrochemical performances required the balance in polarity, porosity and conductivity. At relatively high rate $(5.0\mathrm{~C~}$ and 10.0 C) desirable for fast-charge devices, however, the effect of ion diffusion dominated, where the pore geometries of MOFs became critical. Among these three MOF based electrodes, ppy-S-in-PCN-224 with cross-linked pore and tunnels exhibited the best high rate performance, with the specific capacity of $790~\mathsf{m A h}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ and 640 mAh g-1 after 400-cycle tests at $_{5.0\mathrm{~C~}}$ and $\mathtt{10.0~C}$ , respectively, rivalled that of S-in-carbon, $457\:\mathrm{mAh}\:\mathfrak{g}^{-1}$ at $_{5.0\mathsf{C}}$ under the same condition.
Previously, porous carbon, conductive polymer and MOFs have been tested as sulfur hosts, however, neither of them meet all three criteria when used alone (Scheme 1A and 1B).[5] Due to the flexible design in pore geometries, MOFs provide more structural variety than other materials. Prior to this study, MOFs were used as electrode materials either in pristine form, where the conductivity was limited,[6] or in the form of carbon by pyrolysis, where both the porosity and polarity features were compromised.[7] Therefore, the rate performance of MOF based Li-S battery never went beyond $1.0\mathrm{~C~}$ . In this study, the ppyMOF compartments inherited structural polarity that originated from metal-oxide units across the framework and preserved the high porosity of the parent MOFs (Scheme 1B). These combined with the excellent conductivity provided by ppy and fine-tuned geometry lead to their high specific capacity at previously inaccessible rate of $\mathtt{10.0C}$ .
The typical construction of sulfur containing ppy-MOF compartment was achieved by a three-step method, 1) synthesis and activation of MOFs, 2) impregnation of sulfur into their pores (S-in-MOF), and 3) encapsulation of S-in-MOF particles within ppy layer to generate the final construct (ppy-S-in-MOF) (Figure 1A). First, MOF crystals were synthesized by solvothermal reaction and then activated by solvent removal under vacuum. Powder X-Ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of all three MOFs (MIL-53, MIL-101 and PCN-224) matched well with their simulated patterns, confirming the phase purity (Figure S2-S4). The morphologies of PCN-224 and MIL-101 were cubic and octahedral, respectively, with the particle size ranging from 500 to $800~\mathsf{n m}$ , as revealed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Figure 1B, 1E and S15). Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas of MIL-53, MIL-101 and PCN-224 revealed by ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at $77\mathsf{K}$ were 1370, 3250 and $2660~\mathsf{m}^{2}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, consistent with the reported values (Figure S6- S11).[4]
Second, sulfur molecules were successfully introduced into the MOF pores in a quantitative manner through melt diffusion at $155^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , as reflected in the drastic decrease in ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptakes of these S-in-MOF samples in comparison to the pristine MOFs (Figure S6-S11). The original morphology of MOF crystals was preserved in this process, without any observable sulfur species on the crystal surface (Figure 1C, 1F and Figure S15). The crystallinities of these MOFs were well-maintained as evidenced by the sharp peaks in their PXRD patterns. The absence of sulfur peak $(2\theta=23.2^{\circ})$ and systematic variation in the peak intensities of MOFs confirmed the confinement of sulfur species within the MOF pores (Figure S2-S4).[8] The precise control of sulfur content was achieved by varying the feeding ratio, and the exact loading amount was analysed quantitatively by both elemental analysis (EA) and thermal-gravimetric analysis (TGA) (Figure S12 and Table S1). Given the high porosity of MOFs, sulfur can be introduced at more than $60\%$ by weight.
Third, the final construct, ppy-S-in-MOF, was obtained by encapsulation of S-in-MOF sample using ppy via a solution based chemical oxidative polymerization process. Successful polymerization was reflected by the appearance of characteristic peaks of ppy at 1195 and $917~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ in the Fourier transformed infrared (FTIR) spectrum, representing $C-N$ and $\mathsf{\Gamma}=\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-H}$ bond, respectively (Figure S13).[9] PXRD patterns of the ppy-S-in-MOF samples confirmed the crystallinities of these MOFs after encapsulation. Every each of the MOF crystals was homogeneously wrapped with ppy and their ordered shapes can be clearly identified in the SEM images (Figure 1D and 1G). The thickness of the ppy layer was $45\sim50$ nm, assessed by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) (Figure S14). Furthermore, the even distribution of sulfur inside the MOF and the complete encapsulation of S-in-MOF within ppy layer were demonstrated by the energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) of a ppy-S-in-MOF single crystal (Figure 1H-1L and Figure S16). It is worth noting that this method is applicable for other MOFs and feasible for scale up.
The electrical conductivities of the pristine MOFs were low, ranging from $1.4\times10^{-5}$ to $5.7{\times}10^{-5}\thinspace\mathsf{S}\ \mathsf{m}^{-1}$ as measured by twoelectrode method. When prepared into ppy-S-in-MOF constructs, their conductivities were dramatically improved, $3.1\times10^{1}$ , $1.2\times10^{2}$ and $6.7\ \mathsf{S}\ \mathsf{m}^{-1}$ for ppy-S-in-MIL-101, ppy-S-in-MIL-53 and ppy-S-in-PCN-224, respectively (Figure 1M, Table S2), with a maximum increase, thus fulfilled the conductivity requirement for electrodes. In this study, we choose Li-S battery as an example to illustrate the power of this method for the full access of the porosity and polarity advantages of MOFs in electrochemical applications. We used three electrode materials, fine sulfur particles coated with ppy (ppy-S), sulfur infiltrated porous BP2000 (S-in-carbon) and sulfur loaded MOFs without ppy (S-in-MOFs), for control experiments (Scheme 1B).
The porosity and polarity advantages of ppy-MOF compartments were well-demonstrated in their confinement of polysulfide ions $(\mathsf{S}_{\mathsf{n}}{}^{2-})$ in the pores. Here, we used optically transparent cells to assess the leaching of $\mathsf{S}_{\mathsf{n}}{}^{2-}$ from different electrodes, ppy-S-in-MIL-53, S-in-carbon, ppy-S and S-in-MIL53, during initial discharge-charge process (Figure 1N), where less colour in the electrolyte represents better $\mathsf{S}_{\mathsf{n}}{}^{2-}$ confinement. After one discharge-charge cycle over a period of 8 hours, the colour of electrolyte turned yellow in both S-in-carbon and ppy-S cells, while only slight yellow colour and no colour change were observed in the cell of S-in-MIL-53 without ppy, and in ppy-S-inMOF cell, respectively. This can be attributed to the better confinement of $S_{\mathfrak{n}}{}^{2\mathrm{-}}$ species by polar pore environment of MOFs, and the further block by ppy layer.
The difference between S-in-MOF and ppy-S-in-MOF electrodes in their confinement of $\mathsf{S}_{\mathsf{n}}{}^{2-}$ during cycling process was analysed by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS, Figure S18). The initial charge transfer resistance of S-in-MIL-53 cell was $247~\Omega$ . After charge and discharge for 80 cycles, the resistance increased to $313\ \Omega$ , due to the deposition of insulating species $\ensuremath{\langle\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}}$ and $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ ) on the conductive agent, further confirmed the gradual leaching of $\mathsf{S}_{\mathsf{n}}{}^{2-}$ from S-in-MOF sample, which was consistent with previous reports using pristine MOFs as sulfur host.[10] In contrast, a smaller initial charge transfer resistance was observed in ppy-S-in-MIL-53 due to the improvement of electrical conductivity, further decreased to $113\Omega$ in the second cycle, and remained unaltered through 80 cycles, indicating the absence of $\mathsf{S}_{\mathsf{n}}{}^{2-}$ leaching in ppy-S-inMOF.
Given excellent $\mathsf{S}_{\mathsf{n}}{}^{2-}$ confinement in MOF based electrodes, we further tested their electrochemical performances in Li-S batteries. The specific capacities of ppy-S-in-MOF electrodes were first measured at relatively low rate, from $0.1~\mathsf{C}$ to $1.0\mathrm{~C~}$ , while S-in-MOF, ppy-S and S-in-carbon electrodes were used as control under identical conditions. Ppy-S-in-MIL-53 electrode exhibited discharge capacities of 1420, 1080, 960 and $830~\mathsf{m A h}$ ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ at rate of 0.1, 0.3, 0.5 and $1.0\mathrm{~C~}$ , respectively, much higher than those of the nonconductive S-in-MIL-53 counterparts (Figure 2B, Figure S20a). Their cycling performance at rate of $0.5\mathrm{~C~}$ further confirmed the advance of ppy-S-in-MIL-53, with a high capacity retention of $900\ m\mathtt{A h}\ \mathfrak{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles, $82\%$ higher than that of S-in-MIL-53 electrode (Figure 2C). Similarly, improvement of electrochemical performance was also observed in the case of ppy-S-in-MIL-101 and ppy-S-in-PCN-224 in comparison to their pristine MOF counterparts (Figure S24). These clearly demonstrated the importance to address the conductivity challenge in the electrochemical applications of MOFs. In the control experiments, both the nonporous ppy-S electrode and the nonpolar S-in-carbon electrodes exhibited lower capacity than ppy-S-in-MIL-53 at 1.0 C (Figure S20b), confirming that the porosity and the polarity of the sulfur hosts are also crucial to their performance in Li-S batteries. All three critical aspects, polarity, porosity and conductivity were addressed in ppy-S-in-MOF constructs and resulted in high performance (Figure 2A).
Furthermore, high cycling performance was preserved as the weight percent of sulfur reached up to $80\%$ in ppy-S-in-MIL53 (Figure S22 and Tab. S1). The impact of particle sizes was also investigated. Unlike in previous studies using pristine MOFs as electrodes, where their specific capacities were significantly affected by the particle size,[11] negligible difference in battery performance was observed in this study, when the particle size of ppy-S-in-MOF samples varies from $800~\mathsf{n m}$ to $3~{\upmu\mathsf{m}}$ (Figure S25). The micrometre size of ppy-S-in-MOF particles is favourable for scale up and to access higher tap density in the electrode.
Encouraged by the excellent performance of ppy-S-inMOF constructs at relatively low rate $(<1.0~\mathsf{C})$ , we subjected these materials to much higher charge-discharge rates $(2.0\textrm{\textperthousand}$ $\mathrm{~10.0~C)~}$ to further unveil their advantages for applications demanding fast charging. High discharge capacities were observed in ppy-S-in-PCN-224 electrode, 1330, 890, 790, 750, 710 and $640\ m{\tt A}{\sf h}\ {\sf g}^{-1}$ at the rate of 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0 and 5.0 C, respectively, demonstrating their suitability for high rate tests (Figure 2E). In the cycling test at $5.0\mathrm{~C~}$ , ppy-S-in-PCN-224 exhibited high capacity retention of $780~\mathrm{{mAh}~\mathfrak{g}^{-1}}$ after 400 cycles with an average coulombic efficiency of $99.7\%$ . $74\%$ of the initial capacity was retained, corresponding to a small average decay of ${\sim}0.03\%$ per cycle (Figure 2G).
In order to investigate the impact of pore geometry, we tested the cycling performances of the other two MOF based electrodes at $_{5.0\mathrm{~C~}}$ . The capacity retention of ppy-S-in-MIL-53 and ppy-S-in-MIL-101 were 480 and $330\ m A\mathsf{h}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, much lower than that of ppy-S-in-PCN-224. Given their similarity in electrical conductivity, such obvious discrepancy in electrochemical performance should be mainly induced by their distinct difference in pore geometry, which governed the ion transfer across the pores. Apparently, short ion diffusion pathway and larger pore aperture will allow more ions to pass through the pores for a given time, thus provide higher efficiency for ion transfer. Cross-linked pores and tunnels in PCN-224 not only provide shortest ions diffusion pathways but also offer the largest pore apertures (19.3 Å) among all three MOFs in this study, thus ppy-S-in-PCN-224 exhibited the best ions transfer capability, and it is ideally suited for the high rate tests at $_{5.0\mathrm{~C~}}$ (Figure 3A). For MIL-53, although the discrete channels provide short ion diffusion pathway, due to the small pore aperture of 8.7 Å, its high rate performance was limited (Figure 3B). Large pore sizes (34.0 and 29.0 Å) could be found in the crystal of MIL-101, however, the 3D hierarchical structure is composed of alternate large and small pores, thus longer diffusion pathways imposed on ions. In addition, the presence of small pore aperture (7.0 Å) in between the pores further impeded the ion transfer process and leaded to the lowest electrochemical performance by ppy-Sin-MIL-101 at 5.0 C (Figure 3C). This comparison outlined, for the first time, the importance to optimize pore geometry of sulfur hosts for high rate Li-S batteries (Figure 2D).
In the control experiments, S-in-carbon electrodes were tested at the rate of 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0 and $_{5.0\mathsf{C}}$ (Figure 2E). In addition, cycling test at 5.0 C was also performed (Figure 2G). S-in-carbon retained a specific capacity of $460\:\mathsf{m A h}\:\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ after 400 cycles, which is comparable to that of the state-of-the-art carbon hosts for Li-S batteries.[12] In both the rate capability and cycling stability tests, ppy-S-in-PCN-224 electrode excelled in comparison to S-in-carbon (Figure 2E, 2G). The origin of these differences in performance can be gleaned from their distinct behaviours in the galvanostatic discharge-charge (GDC) curves at $5.0\mathsf{C}.\mathsf{A}$ clear plateau was observed in the discharge curve of ppy-S-in-PCN-224 electrode, while the plateau vanished in that of S-in-carbon sample, indicating slow ion diffusion across the pores in porous carbon. This further confirmed the dominating role of pore geometry in the high rate performance of the host materials in Li-S batteries. Fast ion diffusion was still preserved in ppy-S-in-PCN-224 electrode at the rate of $\mathtt{10.0C}$ , reflected in the presence of discharge plateau in its GDC curve. Cycling test at $\mathtt{10.0~C}$ showed that the initial specific capacity of ppy-S-inPCN-224 electrode was $680\ m{\tt A}{\tt h}\ {\tt g}^{-1}$ , and the capacity retention was 99, 94, 88, 74 and $65\%$ after 200, 400, 600, 800 and 1000 cycles, respectively (Figure 2H). This result stands as a new benchmark of long-cycle performance at high rate in Li-S batteries (Table S3, S4).
For porous materials in battery applications, the pore geometries are critical, because they govern ion diffusion throughout the entire charge-discharge process. Ppy-MOF constructs in this study represent a new way to solve the conductivity issue of MOFs, thus their geometric advantages can be fully accessed.  MOFs with short ion transfer pathway and large pore aperture were identified to be the most suitable for long term cycles at extremely high charge-discharge rate in Li-S battery. This study also sets an example for the utilization of nonconductive MOFs in electrochemical applications.
